Key points in the trace:

1. The crash begins in the kernel (via __pthread_kill) and quickly moves to routines in the Emacs executable. This is the usual “panic‐like” sequence when Emacs detects a serious error.

2. Several of the frames (frames 9–14, for example) are in functions with names starting with “bidi_…” such as bidi_resolve_explicit, bidi_resolve_weak, and bidi_move_to_visually_next. “Bidi” stands for bidirectional text handling. This suggests that the crash may have been triggered during processing of bidirectional (for example, mixed left‑to‑right and right‑to‑left) text. Problems in these parts of the code often show up when Emacs tries to display, iterate, or compute visual ordering of text regions that use bidirectional text properties.

3. The later portions of the trace show invocations of general redisplay functions (like display_line, redisplay_window, redisplay_internal) and functions that process user input (read_char, command_loop). This indicates that the crash occurred during an update of the display, which is a common moment for subtle bugs in layout or text rendering (especially when bidi text is involved).

4. You can also see that the call stack moves into evaluated Lisp code (with frames referencing “eval_last_sexp” and “command_execute”) which means that an Emacs Lisp function was active in the process. This may indicate that either the problematic bidirectional processing was triggered by rendering of such evaluated code or that the crash was triggered from a command that indirectly caused Emacs to update the display in a way that exposed a bug.

What can you do about it?

• If you’re able to reproduce the crash reliably (for example when working with bidirectional text, or during a particular command or window configuration), you may want to try disabling Emacs’ bidirectional reordering to see if that circumvents the problem. This can be done by adding something like
  (setq bidi-display-reordering nil)
to your init file (or evaluating it in your session). (Note that doing so might affect the display of bidirectional text.)

• Make sure you are running the latest stable version of Emacs. Many crashes (especially obscure ones deep in text rendering) get fixed over time.

• If the crash persists and you can reliably reproduce it, consider filing a bug report. Include your Emacs version, operating system details, and the complete crash backtrace. The Emacs developers will need this information to diagnose and fix such problems.

In summary, your backtrace indicates that an error in the bidirectional text handling (or a related display update) led Emacs to trigger an abort. Disabling bidi reordering or updating Emacs may help, and if not, you might want to report the issue.

